whats_new
=========

whats_new is a small web application for publishing project updates and future ideas in a simple and readable way.

The project was created for teams that need a lightweight place where users, developers and stakeholders can see what changed in a project, what may be implemented next, and leave comments or replies without requiring an account.

The goal is not to replace issue trackers, project management tools, GitHub Discussions or release management systems. The goal is to provide a small communication layer between a project and the people interested in its evolution.


PURPOSE

The application provides two main views:

Updates
    Changes that were already implemented in the project.

Futuros
    Ideas, planned improvements and features that may be implemented later.

Entries are organized by date and use a format similar to a concise changelog or commit history.

Each entry contains a type, title and description. Users can open an entry and participate in its discussion.


COMMENTS

Comments are intentionally simple.

Users may comment without creating an account or logging in.

Name, e-mail address and telephone number are optional. When no name is provided, the comment is published as anonymous.

E-mail addresses and telephone numbers are stored only so the project administrator may contact the author when necessary. They are not displayed publicly.

Comments support nested replies, including replies to replies.

The administration interface allows comments and replies to be reviewed, answered and removed.


IMPLEMENTATION

whats_new uses a deliberately small technical stack:

    Python
    Flask
    SQLite

SQLite is provided by the Python standard library.

Flask is the only direct external dependency required by the application.

HTML, CSS, SQL queries and server-side logic are kept together in a small application instead of introducing a larger framework structure.

The database is created automatically when the application starts.

Application data and comments are stored in the same SQLite database.


DESIGN

The project follows a lightweight approach.

The implementation intentionally prefers:

    few lines of code
    few dependencies
    few files
    simple SQL
    server-side rendering
    standard Python facilities
    minimal JavaScript
    minimal configuration

The objective is to keep the application easy to understand, portable and inexpensive to maintain.

There is no ORM, frontend framework, build system, JavaScript package manager or external database server.

This also makes the project easy to adapt. The data model, HTML, CSS and application behavior can be changed directly without having to understand several framework layers or toolchains.


DATABASE

The application uses a single SQLite database named:

    dados.db

The main tables are:

    notas
    comentarios

The notas table stores updates and future items.

The comentarios table stores comments and nested replies.

Replies reference another comment through parent_id, allowing an arbitrary discussion depth.

SQL statements that contain user-provided data use parameterized queries instead of string concatenation.


SECURITY

Public comments do not require authentication.

User-provided text is escaped before being rendered in HTML to reduce the risk of cross-site scripting.

Database inserts and updates use SQLite parameter binding to reduce the risk of SQL injection.

Administrative operations are protected by HTTP Basic Authentication.

The default administrative password is intended only for local development and should be changed before exposing the application publicly.


RUNNING

Install the dependency:

    pip install -r requirements.txt

Start the application:

    python app.py

The application will be available at:

    http://127.0.0.1:5000

Main routes:

    /updates
    /futuros
    /admin

The SQLite database is created automatically on first execution.


CONFIGURATION

The application is designed to require very little configuration.

The administrative password may be changed through the environment variable:

    ADMIN_PASSWORD

Example:

    ADMIN_PASSWORD=my_password python app.py

On Windows Command Prompt:

    set ADMIN_PASSWORD=my_password
    python app.py

On PowerShell:

    $env:ADMIN_PASSWORD="my_password"
    python app.py


PORTABILITY

Because the project uses Python, Flask and SQLite, it can run on common Windows and Linux environments without requiring a separate database server or frontend toolchain.

The small codebase is intentional.

whats_new should remain easy to copy, modify, embed into another project or use as the starting point for a more specific internal tool.

If a feature requires a large dependency or a substantial architectural layer, it should be evaluated against the main purpose of the project: keeping the solution small, understandable and adaptable.


SCOPE

whats_new is intended to solve a narrow problem well.

It is not intended to provide:

    issue tracking
    project management
    user accounts
    complex permissions
    voting systems
    notification infrastructure
    release automation
    repository synchronization
    kanban boards

Those functions are better handled by dedicated tools.

whats_new exists only to make project changes, future ideas and their discussions easy to publish and easy to follow.
